XII. En stemme fra dødsriget
XII. A voice from the underworld

[Den tolvte tavle er et tillæg. Fortællingen om genfærdenes verden begynder i et brudstykke.] Da voddet var trukket gennem bygmesterens bolig og nettet havde taget sit bytte […] sagde han: »Herre, hvad kan jeg gøre […] nu da voddet er trukket gennem bygmesterens bolig og nettet har taget sit bytte […]?« Gilgamesh sagde til ham […] [Omtrent to linjer mangler.] »Gilgamesh […]«.
[The twelfth tablet is an appendix. The story of the world of ghosts begins in a fragment.] When the dragnet had passed through the builder's dwelling and the net had taken its catch […] he said, “Lord, what can I do […] now the dragnet has passed through the builder's dwelling and the net has taken its catch […]?” Gilgamesh said to him […] [About two lines are missing.] “Gilgamesh […].”

»Hvis du nærmer dig […] til templerne […] så tag ikke rent tøj på. Som en mand fra byen skal du […] Smør dig ikke med duftende olie fra krukken, for så samles de om dig ved lugten. Sæt ikke din bue mod jorden, for så kredser de, der blev skudt med bue, omkring dig. Bær ikke en stok i hånden, for så råber de døde, der blev slået, efter dig. Tag ikke sko på fødderne og lad ikke dine trin give genlyd mod jorden. Din kone, som du elsker, må du ikke kysse. Din kone, som du hader, må du ikke straffe. Dit barn, som du elsker, må du ikke kysse. Dit barn, som du hader, må du ikke straffe. Jordens sorg holder dig fast.
“If you draw near […] to the temples […] do not put on clean clothes. Like a man of the city you shall […] Don't anoint yourself with fragrant oil from the jar or its scent will draw them round you. Don't rest your bow on the ground or those shot by the bow will circle you. Don't carry a stick in your hand or the ghosts of those who were beaten will cry out against you. Don't put shoes on your feet or let your footsteps echo on the ground. The wife you love you must not kiss. The wife you hate you must not punish. The child you love you must not kiss. The child you hate you must not punish. The mourning of the earth holds you fast.

Hun, der ligger død,\nhun, der ligger død,\nNinazus mor,\nhun, der ligger død.\nIngen kappe skjuler længere\nhendes smukke skuldre.\nIngen trækker længere ved hendes bryst\nsom ved krukken med fedt [?].«
“She who lies dead,\nshe who lies dead,\nmother of Ninazu,\nshe who lies dead.\nNo cloak now covers\nher beautiful shoulders.\nNo one draws at her breast now\nas at the jar of fat [?].”

Han trak […] til […] og kom til templet. Han tog rent tøj på […] som en mand fra byen […]. Han smurte sig med duftende olie fra krukken, og ved lugten samlede de sig om ham. Han satte buen mod jorden. Ånderne kredsede om ham. De, der var skudt med bue, råbte efter ham. Han bar en stok i hånden, og de døde, der var blevet slået, råbte efter ham. Han tog sko på, og hans trin gav genlyd mod jorden. Han kyssede den kone, han elskede, og straffede den kone, han hadede. Han kyssede det barn, han elskede, og straffede det barn, han hadede. Jordens sorg holdt ham fast:
He drew […] to […] and came to the temple. He put on clean clothes […] like a man of the city […]. He anointed himself with fragrant oil from the jar and its scent drew them round him. He rested the bow on the ground. The spirits circled him. Those shot by the bow cried out against him. He carried a stick, and the ghosts of those beaten cried out against him. He put shoes on and his footsteps echoed on the ground. He kissed the wife he loved and punished the wife he hated. He kissed the child he loved and punished the child he hated. The mourning of the earth held him fast.

Hun, der ligger død,\nhun, der ligger død,\nNinazus mor,\nhun, der ligger død.\nIngen kappe skjuler længere\nhendes smukke skuldre.\nIngen trækker længere ved hendes bryst\nsom ved krukken med fedt [?].
She who lies dead,\nshe who lies dead,\nmother of Ninazu,\nshe who lies dead.\nNo cloak now covers\nher beautiful shoulders.\nNo one draws at her breast now\nas at the jar of fat [?].

Han kaldte på Enkidu, for at han skulle stige op af jorden: »Det var ikke pestguden Namtar, der tog ham, heller ikke feberen. Det var jorden alene. Det var ikke den skånselsløse Nergal, som ligger på lur, der tog ham. Det var jorden alene. Han faldt heller ikke dér, hvor mennesker kæmpede. Det var jorden alene, der tog ham.« Ninsuns søn sørgede over sin tjener Enkidu og gik alene til Ekur, Enlils tempel.
He called for Enkidu to rise from the earth. “It wasn't the plague god Namtar who seized him, nor was it fever. It was the earth alone. It wasn't merciless Nergal, the one who crouches in wait, who seized him. It was the earth alone. Nor did he fall where men fought. It was the earth alone that seized him.” Ninsun's son mourned his servant Enkidu and went alone to Ekur, Enlil's temple.

»Enlil, min far, nu har voddet også slået mig til jorden. Nettet har også slået mig til jorden. Lad Enkidu stige op af jorden, beder jeg dig! Det var ikke pestguden Namtar, der tog ham, heller ikke feberen. Det var jorden alene. Det var ikke den skånselsløse Nergal, som ligger på lur, der tog ham. Det var jorden alene. Han faldt heller ikke dér, hvor mennesker kæmpede. Det var jorden alene, der tog ham.« Men fader Enlil gav intet svar.
“Enlil, my father, now the dragnet has struck me down to earth too. The net has struck me down to earth too. Let Enkidu rise from the earth, I beg you! It wasn't the plague god Namtar who seized him, nor was it fever. It was the earth alone. It wasn't merciless Nergal, the one who crouches in wait, who seized him. It was the earth alone. Nor did he fall where men fought. It was the earth alone that seized him.” But father Enlil gave no answer.

Så gik han til måneguden: »Månegud, min far, nu har voddet også slået mig til jorden. Nettet har også slået mig til jorden. Lad Enkidu stige op af jorden, beder jeg dig! Det var ikke pestguden Namtar, der tog ham, heller ikke feberen. Det var jorden alene. Det var ikke den skånselsløse Nergal, som ligger på lur, der tog ham. Det var jorden alene. Han faldt heller ikke dér, hvor mennesker kæmpede. Det var jorden alene, der tog ham.« Men måneguden gav intet svar.
Then he went to the moon god. “Moon god, my father, now the dragnet has struck me down to earth too. The net has struck me down to earth too. Let Enkidu rise from the earth, I beg you! It wasn't the plague god Namtar who seized him, nor was it fever. It was the earth alone. It wasn't merciless Nergal, the one who crouches in wait, who seized him. It was the earth alone. It wasn't merciless Nergal, the one who crouches in wait, who seized him. It was the earth alone. Nor did he fall where men fought. It was the earth alone that seized him.” But the moon god gave no answer.

Så gik han til Ea: »Ea, min far, nu har voddet også slået mig til jorden. Nettet har også slået mig til jorden. Lad Enkidu stige op af jorden, beder jeg dig! Det var ikke pestguden Namtar, der tog ham, heller ikke feberen. Det var jorden alene. Det var ikke den skånselsløse Nergal, som ligger på lur, der tog ham. Det var jorden alene. Han faldt heller ikke dér, hvor mennesker kæmpede. Det var jorden alene, der tog ham.« Fader Ea hørte ham og sagde til krigeren Nergal: »Nergal, hør mig! Åbn et hul i jorden, så Enkidus ånd kan stige op og tale med sin bror.«
Then he went to Ea. “Ea, my father, now the dragnet has struck me down to earth too. The net has struck me down to earth too. Let Enkidu rise from the earth, I beg you! It wasn't the plague god Namtar who seized him, nor was it fever. It was the earth alone. It wasn't merciless Nergal, the one who crouches in wait, who seized him. It was the earth alone. Nor did he fall where men fought. It was the earth alone that seized him.” Father Ea heard him and said to the warrior Nergal, “Nergal, hear me! Open a hole in the earth so Enkidu's spirit can rise and talk to his brother.”

Krigeren Nergal lyttede til Ea og åbnede et hul i jorden. Enkidus ånd kom op af jorden som en vind. De omfavnede hinanden og […] talte sammen i sorg. »Fortæl mig, min ven. Fortæl, min ven, fortæl mig, hvad du har set af dødsrigets love.« »Nej, min ven. Det vil jeg ikke fortælle dig. Det vil jeg ikke. Hvis jeg fortalte dig, hvad jeg har set af dødsrigets love, satte du dig ned og græd.« »Så lad mig sætte mig ned og græde.«
The warrior Nergal listened to Ea and opened a hole in the earth. Enkidu's spirit came up from the earth like a wind. They embraced and […] spoke together in sorrow. “Tell me, friend. Tell me, friend, please tell me what you've seen of the laws of the underworld.” “No, friend. I won't tell you. I will not. If I told you what I've seen of the laws of the underworld, you'd sit down and weep.” “Then let me sit down and weep.”

»Vennen [?], som du kærtegnede til din glæde: Ormen er trængt ind i hans krop som i en gammel kappe. Bruden [?], som du kærtegnede til din glæde: Hendes krop er fuld af støv […] han talte, og ned i jorden sank han […] han talte, og ned i jorden sank han.« »Den, der faldt i […] så du ham?« »Ja, jeg så […]« [Omkring sytten linjer mangler.] »Som en smuk søjle […] støtter en indre søjlegang [?] […]« [Omkring femogtyve linjer mangler.]
“The friend [?] you caressed to your delight: a worm has entered his body as it would an old cloak. The bride [?] you caressed to your delight: her body is full of dust […] he spoke and sank down into the earth […] he spoke and sank down into the earth.” “The one who fell in […] did you see him?” “Yes, I saw […].” [About seventeen lines are missing.] “Like a fine pillar […] supports an inner portico [?] […].” [About twenty-five lines are missing.]

»Ham, der faldt fra en stang,\nså du ham?« »Ja, jeg så ham.\nStraks for […]\nved at tage en prop ud […].«\n»Ham, som døden […]\nså du ham?« »Ja, jeg så ham.\nHan hviler på et leje.\nKlart vand drikker han.«
“The one who fell from a pole,\ndid you see him?” “Yes, I saw him.\nAt once for […]\nby taking out a plug […].”\n“The one whom death […]\ndid you see him?” “Yes, I saw him.\nHe rests upon a couch.\nHe drinks clear water.”

»Ham, der faldt i kamp,\nså du ham?« »Ja, jeg så ham.\nFar og mor løfter hans hoved.\nHans kone bøjer sig over ham i sorg.«\n»Ham, hvis lig ligger på steppen,\nså du ham?« »Ja, jeg så ham.\nHans ånd finder ingen hvile i jorden.«\n»Ham, hvis ånd ingen tager sig af,\nså du ham?« »Ja, jeg så ham.\nBundsjatter og brødskorper,\nsmidt ud på gaden, lever han af.«
“The one who fell in battle,\ndid you see him?” “Yes, I saw him.\nFather and mother lift his head.\nHis wife bends over him in sorrow.”\n“The one whose corpse lies on the steppe,\ndid you see him?” “Yes, I saw him.\nHis spirit finds no rest in the earth.”\n“The one whose ghost has no one to tend it,\ndid you see him?” “Yes, I saw him.\nDregs and scraps of bread\nthrown in the street are what he lives on.”
